+    <section class="journal" aria-labelledby="journal-title">
+      <p class="journal-eyebrow">From the farm to your first sip</p>
+      <div class="journal-header">
+        <h2 id="journal-title">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+        <button class="btn" id="summarize-journal" type="button" aria-describedby="journal-status"
+          aria-controls="journal-summary" disabled>Summarize</button>
+      </div>
+      <div class="journal-story" id="journal-story">
+        <p>A good cup of coffee begins long before the roaster warms up. It begins with a place: the soil beneath a coffee tree, the shade above it, and the people who care for it through a whole season. When we source beans for The Daily Grind, we look beyond a tasting note or a country on a bag. We want to understand how a coffee was grown, how it was processed, and what makes that particular harvest worth sharing.</p>
+        <p>Our search starts with conversations with growers, cooperatives, and sourcing partners who can trace a lot back to its producer. We ask about harvest conditions, picking practices, and the challenges of the season. A cooperative can connect small farms with the equipment and expertise they need to prepare their coffee for export; a sourcing partner can help us understand a region we cannot visit ourselves. What matters to us is a clear chain of information and a relationship that can grow beyond a single purchase.</p>
+        <p>Then comes the tasting table. We roast small samples and taste them side by side, a process called cupping, to explore sweetness, acidity, body, and finish. We taste again as the cups cool, when a coffee often reveals a different side of itself. Some lots offer the comforting notes of chocolate and toasted nuts that work beautifully in espresso. Others bring delicate fruit or floral notes that shine in a slower brew. We choose for balance and character, rather than expecting every origin to taste the same.</p>
+        <p>Processing is part of that character, too. Washed coffees have their fruit removed before drying, often giving the cup a crisp, clear profile. Natural coffees dry inside the fruit and can develop a fuller, fruitier expression. Neither method tells the whole story: careful fermentation, clean water where washing is used, and even drying all shape the final result. We ask our partners about these steps, alongside soil care, shade management, and the practical realities of growing coffee in a changing climate.</p>
+        <p>Responsible sourcing also means talking about the economics behind the cup. We consider quality, production costs, and the work involved in bringing a harvest to market when discussing a purchase. We ask what price information our partners can share and avoid treating a label as a substitute for those conversations. Our aim is to build dependable relationships, share useful feedback, and return to coffees we love when the next harvest is ready. Good sourcing is ongoing work, with questions to revisit every season.</p>
+        <p>Once the green beans reach our roastery, we develop a roast that brings out their individual strengths. Small test batches help us find the right balance of sweetness and structure without covering up the qualities we selected at the tasting table. We continue tasting after roasting and make adjustments as the coffee changes. By the time those beans reach your cup, our hope is simple: that you can taste both the care at the farm and the attention at the roaster, one thoughtful sip at a time.</p>
+      </div>
